WEBVTT

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.810
Everybody else has been on 
the team since fourth grade!

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.670
Swapping one of them out for me 
when I've only just joined?

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.900
No way! I don't want to be the bad guy!

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.450
I'll come right out and say it.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.820
I <i>need </i>to win two more games
so we can play the Touto Boys!

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.330
Me and Andy together!

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.880
<i>That idiot!</i>

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.520
All right!

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.880
I'm ready for battle!

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.990
The Experience Level of the Catcher

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.810
We're going on ahead, Daigo-kun.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.490
I've left your lunchbox on the table.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.360
Huh?

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.240
Sis, you have a game today, too?

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.450
What?

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.320
I told you I did at dinner last night.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.410
I'm going to go cheer on Izumi this morning,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.250
so unfortunately,
 I won't make it to your game.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.080
Oh, okay.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.650
I'm really sorry.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.880
In a normal household, the father 
would've gone to the other game.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.760
Yeah, too bad Pop's gone AWOL.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.700
Shush.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.350
But I'll be free this afternoon.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.880
Go win your second round game.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.100
I'll be there to cheer you on
 in the third round.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.820
As if the Dolphins' current crop
can make it that far.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:55.850
Shut up!

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:57.350
We will!

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.240
Sure you will.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.500
Later!

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.140
Make sure you lock up.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.750
<i>So nobody's coming to cheer me on?</i>

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.230
<i>Satou-san said he had work today, too.</i>

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.850
<i>Oh well.</i>

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.300
<i>Cheerleaders or no cheerleaders, we'll win!</i>

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.120
<i>We'll trounce them. That'll show Sis.</i>

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.340
If we beat Nijigaoka and 
make it through the second round,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.460
we'll have lunch, then play our 
third-round match in the afternoon.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.030
If all goes to plan, 
it's going to be a tough day,

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.890
so do your best out there!

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.010
Yes, sir!

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.940
Uh, if we win both games, 
are we going for barbecue again?

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.700
Ariyoshi

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.700
Matsubara

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.830
Isn't the answer to that obvious?

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:46.990
All right!

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.450
Let's win and celebrate with some tasty meat!

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.790
Yeah!

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.920
Mifune Dolphins 
Manager

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.920
Tashiro

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.290
Hey! I can't take you 
all out after every game!

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.500
I'd go bankrupt!

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.250
Pfft, stop getting ahead of yourselves.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.250
Mifune Dolphins
Coach

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.250
Fujii

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.220
Satou Hikaru

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.220
Sakura Mutsuko

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.440
You guys really love barbecue, don't ya?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.220
You said it!

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.220
Urabe Hayato

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:10.720
Hey.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.590
Andy!

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:13.600
What are you doing here?

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:14.900
You can sit on the bench already?

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:20.500
Yeah, my injury's not so bad. 
The cane's just a precaution.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.860
Suzuki Andy

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.860
I can still be a pinch hitter if needed.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:23.900
Don't be stupid.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.250
I won't allow you to take that kind of risk.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.860
I promise we'll win both games today.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.790
So just sit tight and get ready 
for the real challenge next week!

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.620
I'll now read out the starting lineup 
for our second-round game.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.120
Batting first, shortstop, Kimura.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.100
Yes, sir!

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.180
Batting second, catcher, Shigeno.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:44.960
Yes, sir!

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.370
Batting third, pitcher, Urabe.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.220
Yes, sir.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.260
<i>Looks like it's our regular lineup.</i>

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.970
<i>Which means our cleanup is...</i>

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.970
Batting fourth, center field, Satou.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:54.980
Yes, sir!

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:55.990
<i>Seriously?!</i>

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.720
Wow! You're batting fourth!

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.830
What's the big deal?

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.730
It just means I'm fourth up
to the plate, right?

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.950
Batting fifth, third base, Ariyoshi.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:04.740
Yes, sir!

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.900
Batting sixth, left field, Kishimoto.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.740
Kishimoto

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:07.740
Yes, sir!

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:09.740
<i>Please...</i>

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.780
Batting seventh, first base, Matsubara.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.670
Yes, sir!

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:15.250
<i>Please don't pick me.</i>

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.510
Batting eighth, second base, Katsumata.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:18.290
Yes, sir!

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:21.440
Batting ninth, right field, Sakura.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.860
Nagai, Tsuburaya, and Takahashi
 are on the bench.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.260
That's everything.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:32.220
Nagai

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.360
I've... been benched?

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.520
I'm playing... right field?

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.360
<i>I see.</i>

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.100
<i>I knew he would.</i>

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.020
<i>The manager realized Sakura
 had screwed up on purpose.</i>

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.240
Line up.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.320
Okay, let's go!

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.350
Yeah!

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.420
<i>He's huge...</i>

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:01.960
Play!

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.520
Good luck!

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.360
<i>I don't believe this.</i>

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:07.770
<i>What's going on?</i>

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.010
<i>Why me?</i>

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.520
Let's do our best, Mutsuko-chan!

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:13.850
Uh, yeah!

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.080
M-Manager?

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:16.850
What is it?

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:20.020
Um, how come I'm on the bench?

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.710
Sakura was making errors all over 
the field in practice yesterday.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.940
They weren't errors.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:26.860
Huh?

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.960
She was sandbagging 
because she's only just joined,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.200
and she thought that would 
keep her out of the lineup.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:35.520
But she's got a real talent for batting.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.620
An almost prodigious talent...

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.120
So she's better than me?

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.110
I'm sorry, Nagai.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.000
I'll make sure you get your chance.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.600
Takahashi

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.600
Tsuburaya

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.050
This doesn't sit right.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.890
It's the sixth graders'
last summer, after all...

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:03.150
It's the top of the first,
and the Nijigaoka Beetles are in at bat.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.440
Batting first for them is 
center fielder, Okegawa-kun.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.530
Oh? So from round two onward, 
you get an announcer, huh?

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.390
<i>So that's his son, huh?</i>

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:17.240
Nijigaoka Beetles
Manager

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:17.240
Komori

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.240
<i>He's looking pretty nervous out there.</i>

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:19.980
Hey! Rookie catcher!

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:21.970
C'mon, start talking!

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:23.210
The game's underway!

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:26.920
L-Let's do this!

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.820
Play ball!

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:33.520
<i>Pitcher: Urabe Hayato.</i>

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.110
<i>Throw: Right overhand.</i>

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:36.680
<i>Pitches: Around 100 kmph.</i>

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.180
<i>Control and stability: Both B+.</i>

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.760
<i>However, he has issues with stamina.</i>

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:46.860
<i>Starting with a bunt?</i>

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:50.250
Strike!

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.260
They're already messing with us?

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.210
I guess we should have expected that 
with Komori as their manager.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:57.660
They have a shrewd offense.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:00.610
Oh, Komori!

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.580
I remember when he used 
to be the Dolphins' manager.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:09.120
<i>He might be playing a different position,</i>

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.340
<i>but he reminds me a lot 
of a young Honda-kun.</i>

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:16.800
<i>When I was little, everybody made 
fun of me for being an introvert.</i>

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:22.660
<i>But when I met Honda-kun and started 
playing baseball with the Dolphins,</i>

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.230
<i>my life changed in a big way.</i>

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:30.060
<i>To this day, I'm proud of the fact that,
 during grade and middle school,</i>

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.600
<i>I was in a battery with Honda-kun.</i>

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.030
<i>But...</i>

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.400
<i>I'm sorry, Honda-kun.</i>

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:38.980
<i>I still have a soft spot for the Dolphins,</i>

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:41.400
<i>because I used to manage them, 
and your kid's there now...</i>

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:45.220
<i>But I won't be showing them any mercy.</i>

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:47.380
<i>This game is ours. Just you wait and see.</i>

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:51.820
Ball.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.470
<i>So they're just trying to trip me up, huh?</i>

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:00.550
Man, it looks like Komori's 
become a proper old fart.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:03.560
You could say he's got <i>personality</i>.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.090
Wait, aren't we in trouble here?

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:10.770
The Dolphins were stronger 
when he was manager.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:16.200
Doesn't that basically mean 
he's a better manager than you?

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.970
Well, he's not my favorite 
opponent, that's for sure.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.920
And of course, there was 
that incident in high school.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.660
Huh? What incident?

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.090
You don't remember?

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:30.460
How we came up against him in 
the Prefectural Championships?

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.760
You made so many errors, 
we almost handed the game to them.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.970
<i>You </i>know! The game against Mifune!

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:49.350
Don't dredge up memories of my dark past!

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.400
<i>Jeez...</i>

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.860
<i>Why am I even in the starting lineup?</i>

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.580
<i>Everyone hates me now!</i>

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:02.480
<i>Third!</i>

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:05.080
I don't think so!

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.250
Safe!

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:11.590
Urabe, what are you playing at?!

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:13.230
Nice bunt!

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.090
Nice bunt, nice bunt!

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:17.180
Damn.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.370
Shake it off!

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.470
Daigo!

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.670
That was supposed to go to third!

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:24.470
As catcher, you're in the best 
position to call that. Why didn't you?!

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:25.950
M-My bad!

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:29.610
Batting second, shortstop,

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:30.900
Ageo-kun.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.440
<i>Another lefty.</i>

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.390
Curse him.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:35.110
Huh?

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.690
Their manager used to be a catcher.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:39.950
He's using a small-ball strategy.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:45.100
I don't know if an inexperienced 
catcher like Daigo can handle it.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.550
Huh? A strategy to counter steals?

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:02.690
Yeah. It's the area that worries 
me most in the next game.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:06.820
Your arm isn't gonna give 
them a free pass, is it?

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:08.980
A "free pass"?

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.980
Letting runners steal bases easily.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:14.110
Did Satou-san teach you how to stop runners?

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:17.820
Huh? Uh, yeah, we discussed it briefly.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:19.520
He said there are
 little tricks that can help,

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:23.370
but I can just use the same principles 
as throwing the ball from the outfield.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:25.680
Really? That'll work?

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.910
Well, I haven't tried it out yet.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:30.500
You haven't even practiced doing it?!

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:33.000
How do you expect to 
play catcher in a game?!

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:36.060
Let's go find a wide-open space 
and practice right now!

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:41.910
Preventing steals is a joint 
effort by pitcher and catcher.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:46.140
I'll do my best to pick off 
runners when I can, too.

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:49.550
Safe!

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.660
I'm not that good at the slide step, 
but I'll practice it from now till the game.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.030
After that, it's up to you.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.290
Anyway, the start of the game is key!

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.590
If they sniff weakness from the offset,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.320
our opponents will take advantage 
and run every time.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:10.410
Nijigaoka Beetles 
Pitcher

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:10.410
Tamaki

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:08.100
Are we going to let the boys run, Manager?

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:09.260
You bet.

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:14.080
Their catcher's a rookie, 
and we know he has a weak arm.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:19.380
You'd need players as good as the 
Touto Boys to stop Okegawa in his tracks.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:22.550
He's running!

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.050
Listen up, Daigo-kun.

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:29.330
Once you're able to throw 
to home from the outfield,

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:32.040
you'll have nothing to fear from 
throwing the ball to second

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.350
when you're wearing the mask.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.590
But Satou-san, when I'm 
throwing from the outfield,

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:42.740
I can use the momentum of my run
 to make up for my weak arm.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:45.220
As a catcher, I can't do that.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:48.310
Yeah, that's true. For <i>most</i> people, that is.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:49.080
Huh?

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:52.450
What I mean is, you should just run.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:55.460
Run the way even a catcher can.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.240
Safe!

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:10.430
So close.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:15.020
That catcher had to bounce it
 just to get the ball to second.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:16.120
Talk about weak!

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:18.260
Looks like running will be 
a cinch is this game.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.240
What game are you idiots watching?

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.000
That was Okegawa running.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.360
They almost had him, 
so anyone else would've been out.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:30.980
If the ball had bounced a little lower, 
even Okegawa would've been in trouble.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.370
It was a good throw.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:38.280
<i>Looks like his arm isn't a free pass.</i>

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.040
<i>That wasn't bad for a novice, Daigo-kun.</i>

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:44.740
Let's stay focused!

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.840
<i>But how long can you keep up with us?</i>

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:48.650
<i>Damn.</i>

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:50.400
<i>That was really close.</i>

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:54.190
When throwing to second, you put your 
left foot forward, bend your knees,

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:55.720
and catch the ball in this stance.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:14:00.360
Then, you'd normally take a step 
and release it, but...

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:04.340
Instead, you're going to catch the ball
 with your right foot forward.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.210
If you then take a big step 
forward with your left foot,

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:09.610
the momentum you generate
is similar to a running throw.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:14.020
If you can make a low and
accurate throw to the base,

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:16.370
you can catch most steals.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.680
Yes, sir!

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:20.740
<i>I'll show them next time.</i>

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:23.250
<i>No outs, runner on second base.</i>

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.720
<i>In this situation, we've got 
a fifty percent chance of scoring,</i>

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:32.360
<i>but if we can get the runner to third,
that chance rises to ninety percent.</i>

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:37.180
Oh, no, you don't!

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:38.620
F-First!

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.350
Safe!

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:46.640
All right! All safe!

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.320
No outs, runners on first and third!

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:50.900
Shake it off.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:53.110
Shake <i>what</i> off?

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:56.040
You're one beat too slow with your calls!

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.920
Not to mention, I could've 
probably made the throw to third.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:02.610
I should've just thrown it.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.960
Batting third, third base, Fukaya-kun.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:10.970
Nothing to do, huh?

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:13.970
All this time, the ball's 
stayed in the infield.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:15.510
<i>Damn it.</i>

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:18.200
<i>They're not getting the first run, no way!</i>

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:20.440
He's running!

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:21.320
A squeeze!

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.610
No, it may just be a steal!

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:26.610
<i>If they throw to second, 
make a dash for home, Okegawa!</i>

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:27.750
<i>You think I'll let you steal?</i>

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:29.150
<i>This time, you're out.</i>

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:29.610
Ball.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:31.390
Don't throw it!

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:32.860
The runner on third's stealing home!

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:37.740
Runners on second and third!

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.810
<i>That was close.</i>

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:43.020
<i>Their regular catcher spotted our play.</i>

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.500
<i>But now, our chance of scoring 
more than two runs has increased.</i>

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:50.490
Tashiro, shouldn't we call a timeout?

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:53.300
Our battery's unsteady from the get-go.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:55.740
It's not that they're unsteady...

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.060
This is just how strong they are right now.

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.490
When it comes to their tactics,

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:06.020
it's not as if they're doing 
anything vastly different to normal.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:10.970
This battery—no, Daigo—just isn't 
able to keep up with the game.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:16.710
Catcher is a thinker's position 
that requires a lot more anticipation

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:18.280
and decision making than any other.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:21.630
This knowledge can only be 
gained through experience.

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:24.410
If the bench dictates 
every move he makes...

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:25.380
<i>A squeeze!</i>

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.340
...he'll never become a true catcher.

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:37.180
Safe!

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:40.180
Wait! Don't throw it!

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:40.890
Huh?

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.200
Too late.

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:45.690
Out.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.060
Matsubara! Back home!

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:49.770
Safe!

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:53.610
Nijigaoka

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:53.610
Mifune

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.610
We're already two runs ahead!

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:57.840
What? They got two runs?

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.830
Without any hits?

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.610
<i>It's unexpected, but we've already 
got two runs on the board.</i>

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.840
<i>If their defense at the plate stays 
that loose, we'll score another three.</i>

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:11.120
Damn.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:13.010
Hey, give me the ball.

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:15.620
I-I'm sorry.

00:17:15.620 --> 00:17:16.740
Stop that.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.520
Letting a runner get on base 
was my fault, too.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:25.310
And I can't expect someone with 
no experience to handle a double squeeze.

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:27.850
I don't need apologies.

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:29.560
Just keep learning the ropes.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:32.670
As catcher, you've got so much 
to do and to think about,

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:35.570
you don't have time to get down on yourself.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:36.810
Right!

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:41.080
Batting fourth, pitcher, Tamaki-kun.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.390
No runners, huh? That's boring.

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:47.360
Guess I'll just have to make this a homer.

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:50.760
Hey, Momoko!

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:53.170
Over here! Here!

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:56.170
Why are you hiding over in this corner?

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:59.470
Let's get up close to watch our grandson.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.080
Don't be silly. If he saw his doddery 
old grandparents in the crowd,

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.480
he'd probably tense up!

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:09.020
Daigo's not as brutishly coarse 
as Goro. He's delicate.

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:11.830
So we should stay hidden 
when we're watching over him.

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.190
I wonder if that's right...

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:16.500
Strike!

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:17.820
Nice one!

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.780
He's doing good!

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:24.050
Wow, he really <i>is</i> wearing the mask.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:28.210
Nijigaoka

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:28.210
Mifune

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:28.210
What?! They're already two runs behind?!

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:36.010
Yup, <i>that's</i> going deep to left 
center and <i>I'm</i> going to second.

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:42.510
Huh?

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:44.260
He caught it!

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:46.020
Nice, center!

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.540
Nice catch, Hikaru-kun!

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:52.230
Nah, I just got pumped because 
the ball finally came my way.

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:54.880
That center fielder's good.

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:57.460
<i>So that's Satou Toshiya's son?</i>

00:18:58.060 --> 00:18:59.620
<i>I don't have a lot of data on him,</i>

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:02.290
<i>but I do know he was the 
relief pitcher in their first game.</i>

00:19:02.290 --> 00:19:03.940
<i>He's an outstanding talent.</i>

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:08.880
<i>He's the one player on the Dolphins 
we need to watch out for.</i>

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:12.940
Strike!

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:14.590
Three outs! Change sides!

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:17.600
Phew, they're finally changing sides.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:19.880
We got away with only conceding two runs.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:24.990
But I'm really glad to see 
Daigo's come back here.

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:25.960
Me, too.

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:31.310
Hey, whose grave is this?

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:35.060
Honda Family

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:35.060
"Hon-da..." Who's that?

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:36.950
Goro's father.

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:41.140
Or, in other words, your and 
your sister's real grandfather.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.640
"Real grandfather"?

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.200
So does that mean you're a fake grandfather?

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:51.410
Jeez! I showed you the photo 
and explained it to you already.

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:54.960
Your pop's father was involved 
in an on-field accident,

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.450
and died a long time ago.

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:58.760
Huh.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:01.420
So this grandpa was a baseball player, too?

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:02.600
That's right.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:07.280
Both of your grandfathers 
were pro baseball players.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:12.350
You'll also join a baseball team when you 
reach fourth grade, won't you, Dai-chan?

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:17.020
Yeah! I'm <i>definitely </i>going to 
be a baseball player, too!

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:19.620
That's what he said back then,

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:24.110
so it really bummed me out when he quit 
baseball not long after picking up the bat.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.400
It looks like he's made some good friends.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:32.100
And these friends have 
brought him back to the game.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:35.580
<i>Bottom of the first, Dolphins at bat.</i>

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:38.380
Kimura

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.380
<i>Batting first, shortstop, Kimura-kun.</i>

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:49.880
According to Urabe, he's an Eephus 
pitcher who mainly throws slower balls.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:53.160
That's why we practiced
hitting pitches at half speed.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:55.560
What's an Eephus again?

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:58.920
Put simply, it's an extremely slow pitch.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:02.150
I wonder what his throw will be like.

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:03.190
Play!

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:11.910
That's mega slow!

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:14.910
Strike!

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.400
That's even slower than I imagined!

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.880
But it's just slow, nothing more.

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:21.960
If he got a no-hitter with those pitches,

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.510
their opposition in the first round
must've been very weak.

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:30.150
That's some relaxed pitching
 from such a big youngster.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.530
With throws like those, they'll claw
back those two runs in no time.

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:37.940
No, if the balls were just slow, 
they'd be easy to hit.

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:41.060
But with a slow rainbow pitch like that,

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:45.190
the impact point becomes tiny, 
so getting a hit is quite difficult.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:48.260
Strike two!

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:52.470
And because they use a softer ball, 
even if you do make contact,

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:57.510
it won't go far unless you hit 
the ball pretty much dead center.

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:01.490
Out.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:03.310
He's good.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:06.360
That was a hard hit to field.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.470
Well, they do have Komori as their manager.

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:10.620
It's not surprising
their defense is so solid.

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:14.330
Batting second, catcher,

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:15.480
Shigeno-kun.

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:18.520
He's up! There's our Dai-chan!

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:21.020
Good luck, Daigo-kun!

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:06.550
The Dolphins just can't seem to score
against the Beetles' iron-tight defense.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:10.420
And in addition to that slow ball, 
their ace has another secret weapon?

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:12.470
Is that even fair?!

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:14.550
If this keeps up, we're in trouble.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:17.180
We've got to do something to break through.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:20.520
Next time: "The Key to Success."

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:25.020
The Key to Success

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:23.150
Run to the stage of your dreams!
